  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Responding to Critical WASH Needs of Conflict- and Climate-Affected Communities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under Lebanon Humanitarian Fund (LHF) 1st Standard Allocation (United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs  - UNOCHA project) Oxfam has two projects; LHF 1;  Oxfam will equip two boreholes in Lebanon (one in Bekaa and one in Beirut) with solar water pumping system. The project title; sustainable safe access to water for Lebanese and refugees in Ain Baalbek and Bchetfine, Chouf LHF 2: Equipping Water Pumping Stations with Solar Systems in the water deprived areas of Bakhaoun and Harf Es Siyad in Donnieh and Daraiya in Chouf for 16,147 vulnerable Lebanese and Syrian refugees and 6 Public Schools, while improving access to Menstrual Hygiene Management to 4,801 women and girls United Nations Children&apos;s Fund (UNICEF) project  - R10839: The proposed intervention responds to immediate Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) needs in an age and gender -sensitive, inclusive and participatory way, and complements existing Protection and Public Health Promotion programmes by Oxfam and local partners in the target area. This maximises assistance to target populations through the provision of lifesaving WASH and Protection services.  By adopting a community -based approach, Oxfam will enable communities to influence decision -making around the intervention, through multiple modalities: 1. Community consultations on activities, specs, locations, topics for hygiene promotion, etc.  2. Real -time generation of perceptions and feedback through digital technology. Oxfam will capitalize on the Community Perceptions Tracker (CPT), a tool that allows the maximisation of cost -effectiveness where programmes respond to people&apos;s immediate and most pressing needs . 3. Support to community structures and community action plans, specifically of Women&apos;s platforms.   Oxfam will ensure that people have safe access to water as per sector standards 35L/d/p, committing to 20 L/p/d as the minimum acceptable threshold (Oxfam&apos;s protocol for trucking ranges is 10 -35 L/d/p depending on avail</narrative>
  </description>
